WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.474
VRAM is normally local to each GPU.

00:00:03.474 --> 00:00:10.217
Installing two cards with 12 gigabytes each does not automatically create one transparent 24-gigabyte device.

00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:16.143
The runtime must support splitting or parallelism for a model to use both.

00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:20.434
Layer splitting places different model layers on different GPUs.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:27.177
A request moves through those layers, so the boundary between devices can add communication overhead.

00:00:27.177 --> 00:00:31.468
Uneven splits may be useful when memory sizes differ.

00:00:31.468 --> 00:00:36.576
Tensor parallelism divides mathematical operations across GPUs at a high level.

00:00:36.576 --> 00:00:42.093
It can use several devices simultaneously but requires synchronization and fast communication.

00:00:42.093 --> 00:00:46.385
Benefits depend on model, runtime, topology, and batch behavior.

00:00:46.385 --> 00:00:51.493
Model sharding is the broader idea of dividing weights or components.

00:00:51.493 --> 00:01:00.279
CPU offload can place some layers or cache in system memory when GPU capacity is insufficient, trading speed for fit.

00:01:00.279 --> 00:01:02.936
These methods are not equivalent.

00:01:02.936 --> 00:01:10.905
Context memory and key-value cache can be large, especially with long prompts, multiple sessions, or certain model architectures.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:18.057
A weight split that barely fits may leave no room for the intended context or concurrency.

00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:22.756
The smallest, slowest, or least-connected GPU can become a bottleneck.

00:01:22.756 --> 00:01:28.274
Balance should consider usable memory, compute, bandwidth, thermal behavior, and software capability.

00:01:28.274 --> 00:01:32.973
Benchmark the exact split rather than adding VRAM labels together.

00:01:32.973 --> 00:01:37.264
Cache placement can be as important as weight placement.

00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:46.460
Long context and simultaneous sessions may load one device unevenly or require transfers, so test the intended cache strategy and concurrency.
